### What is Exchanger?

You create one with `Exchanger<T> ex = new Exchanger<>()`. The only essential method is `exchange(V x)`, which blocks until the other thread also calls exchange, then returns the other's object.

### How does double buffering work?

```java
package com.bank.buffer;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.Exchanger;

public class DoubleBufferDemo {

    private static final int CAPACITY = 100;
    private static final Exchanger<List<String>> exchanger = new Exchanger<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Thread(() -> filler()).start();
        new Thread(() -> drainer()).start();
    }

    static void filler() {
        List<String> buffer = new ArrayList<>(CAPACITY);
        try {
            for (int i = 0; i < 500; i++) {
                buffer.add("Trade-" + i);
                if (buffer.size() == CAPACITY) {
                    buffer = exchanger.exchange(buffer);  // hand full, get empty
                    buffer.clear();
                }
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }

    static void drainer() {
        List<String> buffer = new ArrayList<>(CAPACITY);
        try {
            for (int round = 0; round < 5; round++) {
                buffer = exchanger.exchange(buffer);  // hand empty, get full
                buffer.forEach(trade -> System.out.println("Posted: " + trade));
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }
}
```

### How does the timed variant protect against stalls?

```java
try {
    buffer = exchanger.exchange(buffer, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
} catch (TimeoutException e) {
    System.err.println("Partner stalled, raising alert.");
}
```

### How does Exchanger compare to BlockingQueue?

| Feature | Exchanger | BlockingQueue |
|---------|-----------|---------------|
| Threads | Exactly two | Many producers and consumers |
| Direction | Bidirectional swap | One-directional |
| Internal storage | None | Bounded or unbounded buffer |
| Coupling | Tight (rendezvous) | Loose (decoupled) |
| Buffer reuse | Yes (two objects bounce) | No (elements flow through) |

### What about memory visibility?

Exchange establishes a happens-before relationship. Everything written before exchange is visible to the partner after exchange returns.

### What are common mistakes?

Using Exchanger with more than two threads leads to unpredictable pairing.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can you use Exchanger for more than two threads?
- How does double buffering reduce GC pressure?
- When is BlockingQueue a better choice?
